<h2>How AI Reads This Wiki — the Machine-Readable Layer and Graph Traversal</h2>
<p>One of this wiki's ultimate goals is to make AI <strong>look here first when you hand it a task</strong>, and to have it <strong>analyze along the relationships</strong> rather than searching page by page. That is why, separately from the human-facing screens, there is a <strong>machine-readable layer</strong> that an external AI can discover and traverse on its own. The foundation of this layer is that document bodies are static fragments fetched straight from a URL without JS (<a href="#!kgs-overview">Part 1</a>).</p>

<h3>Three Doors — Cover · Graph · Contract</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong><code>llms.txt</code> (the cover)</strong> — the AI-facing front door at the site root. It lays out the wiki's purpose, where the machine files live, and <strong>the title, summary, and URL of every document</strong>, organized by System. Because it follows the AI ecosystem's convention (llms.txt), it is the first file a newly arrived agent reads.</li>
    <li><strong><code>data/knowledge-graph.json</code> (the self-contained graph)</strong> — <strong>fetch this one file</strong> and you can traverse the entire graph. Every node (document) inlines its title, summary, concepts, <strong>related edges (<code>related</code>)</strong>, and <strong>directed typed relations (<code>relations</code>)</strong>, and <strong>even the URL of the source document</strong>, so there is nothing to join against another file.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="#!ai-guide">AI Traversal Guide</a> (the contract)</strong> — the document that spells out "what a node is, how to interpret an edge, and in what order to fetch." The schema and the rules are pinned here, so every agent reads the wiki the same way.</li>
</ul>

<p>Alongside these, <strong>supporting entry points that back up the three doors</strong> are generated as well. <strong><code>llms-full.txt</code></strong> is a full-corpus dump that packs the plain-text body of every document into a single file, so one fetch loads the whole wiki into context; <strong><code>feed.xml</code> (an Atom feed)</strong> announces the 30 most recent documents in chronological order so human RSS readers and crawlers can subscribe to new posts; and <strong><code>robots.txt</code></strong> names the major AI crawlers (GPTBot, ClaudeBot, PerplexityBot, and others) to welcome them explicitly and advertises the entry points above. The content license is <strong>CC BY 4.0</strong> — robots.txt, llms.txt, the feed, and the site footer consistently declare the same legal signal, "cite and reuse freely as long as you credit the source," so AI systems can quote this wiki with confidence.</p>

<h3>A door for visitors that do not run JavaScript — static snapshots</h3>
<p>Every entry point so far hands machines <strong>data</strong>. Ordinary search engines, however, index <strong>pages</strong>, not data. This wiki is a hash-routed SPA, so a crawler that does not execute JavaScript arrives at <code>#!welcome</code> and sees an <strong>empty shell</strong>. The document fragments (<code>docs/en/…</code>) are the body text, but they are HTML chunks without a title, description, or canonical address — not quite pages.</p>
<p>So each document also ships a <strong>static snapshot</strong> — <code>/p/&lt;name&gt;/</code> (English at <code>/p/en/&lt;name&gt;/</code>, Japanese at <code>/p/ja/&lt;name&gt;/</code>). It carries the same body, topped with a <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> (the first h2), a description (the summary), a <code>canonical</code> address, language cross-links (hreflang), and Article JSON-LD including the license and dates — a <strong>real page</strong>. The <code>#!</code> links inside the body are rewired to sibling snapshots, so a JS-less crawler can <strong>traverse the entire wiki by following links</strong>, and the <strong>hub pages</strong> at <code>/p/</code>, <code>/p/en/</code> and <code>/p/ja/</code> are where that walk starts (the home page builds its navigation in JavaScript, so its raw HTML contains no document links at all — a <code>&lt;noscript&gt;</code> link to the hub removes that dead end).</p>
<p>A snapshot is <strong>a page people are meant to land on</strong>. An earlier version bounced human visitors to the SPA, which threw away the very thing the snapshots exist to provide: a <strong>stable per-document address</strong>. Someone arriving from a search result or a shared link watched the URL turn into <code>/#!name</code>, so bookmarking or re-sharing no longer pointed at that document. It no longer redirects; there is just an &ldquo;open it in the wiki&rdquo; link at the top. Snapshots also carry <strong>Open Graph and Twitter card</strong> tags, so a shared link unfurls with its title, summary and image — the services that build those previews do not run JavaScript, which makes this <strong>something only the static pages can do</strong>.</p>
<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Three surfaces of the same knowledge</span>
    A document has three addresses — <code>url</code> (the raw fragment, for machine parsing), <code>route</code> (the SPA, for people), and <code>page</code> (the static snapshot, for search engines and JS-less crawlers). All three sit in the graph node, so a consumer picks the surface that fits its purpose. What the sitemap advertises is <code>page</code>.
</div>

