<p>Take a real two-hop traversal as an example: starting from the hub <a href="#!hns-install">Running the Harness</a>, one hop reaches 4 neighbors, and two hops reach <strong>6 documents</strong>. From the set of documents reached this way, AI can analyze while explaining "why these documents belong together," grounded in the <strong>shared concepts (shared)</strong> — a <strong>relationship-based</strong> reading that page-by-page search cannot achieve. Add <code>relations</code> and it goes beyond a bare "these are related" to read <strong>the type and direction of the relationship, such as prerequisite, implements, or evidence</strong>, so it can pin down "what to read first" and "which document is the evidence for which claim" in its analysis.</p>

<h3>Never Out of Sync — the Drift Gate</h3>
<p>A machine-readable file that has drifted from the actual knowledge is worse than none at all. So these files are not written by hand; they are <strong>generated automatically from the knowledge index</strong> (robots.txt and the feed are generated artifacts too), and CI's <code>--check</code> gate enforces "no merge if you add or edit a document without regenerating." Even the timestamps in the feed come from git history (<code>doc-dates.json</code>) rather than the build time, so regenerating never shakes the result. The outcome: <strong>the moment a document is added, llms.txt, llms-full.txt, the feed, the graph JSON, the sitemap, and the static snapshots are always brought up to date together</strong>. The snapshot gate goes one step further and also catches <strong>stale snapshots</strong> that are no longer produced — when a document is renamed, the old page would otherwise linger like a ghost and stay exposed to search engines. At validation time we mechanically confirmed that the URLs of all 97 nodes match real files and that broken references (dangling) number 0.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">When you hand a task to AI</span>
    One line in the prompt is enough — <em>"Read dewytear.github.io/llms.txt first, then analyze by following the relationships in knowledge-graph.json (related, relations, concepts)."</em> Schema details and traversal rules are guaranteed as a contract by the <a href="#!ai-guide">AI Traversal Guide</a>. Thanks to this knowledge-management structure, the bigger the wiki grows, the wider AI's analysis radius automatically grows with it.
</div>
